From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Gino D." <25200363+zinosaure@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 17:26:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gameflex 0085: an M0-indexed VGPR access goes through a local
 array

V_MOVRELS_B32 read VGPR[base + M0] by comparing M0 with every register
from the base up to the shader's last one and selecting among them;
V_MOVRELD_B32 rewrote each of those registers with a select. M0 is one
value for the whole wave and the console touches a single register:
Astro Bot's foliage vertex shaders paid ~2 500 comparisons and as many
selects a vertex (22 indexed reads over ~116 registers), and RADV kept
the comparison masks in scalar registers it had to spill (2 313 spills
in the heaviest one): 7.7 ns a vertex against 1 ns for an ordinary
vertex shader, the garden's geometry pass 26 ms of the GPU's 78.

The registers stay SSA values. An indexed access now names its element
(VgprIndexBegin: M0, or 0 past the shader's registers, as before), puts
the registers it may reach one by one (VgprIndexPut), then reads the
element (VgprIndexGet) or writes it where EXEC allows and reads the
registers back (VgprIndexSet, VgprIndexTake). The SPIR-V goes through a
local array in parts of at most 64 registers: RADV keeps such a part in
registers and turns the indexed access into a binary tree of branches;
a larger array goes to scratch memory.

Two earlier forms of this commit, never delivered, kept the indexed
registers themselves in a private array for the whole shader. The
first, one array, put 128 registers in scratch memory (32 KiB a wave);
the second, in parts, had none. Both had the GPU reset when the game
entered a level: behind the array, EliminateReadLane no longer saw a
V_READLANE reading back what a V_WRITELANE had put in a lane (the guest
compiler keeping scalar registers in vector lanes), and two vertex
shaders compiled right before the reset kept some 70 more cross-lane
reads than with 0084, reading lanes a partial wave may not have.

The garden's heaviest vertex shader, compiled by RADV for the 780M on a
null device (Mesa 25.0) from the SPIR-V of the select chains rewritten
this way: 88 914 estimated cycles instead of 226 281 (92 110 with an
essay bounding the chains to 32 registers, wrong for a shader indexing
further), 168 VGPRs instead of 256, no scratch.

At the bench, production settings: the garden 12.06 -> 15.95 and 16.06
images/s over two games, the GPU 81 -> 61.4 ms a frame, the heaviest
foliage draw 2.29 -> 1.06 ms, same image; every module the two versions
share keeps the same cross-lane reads. Tekken 8's menu 18.80 -> 21.47
(two of its pixel shaders index registers), Clair Obscur 14.77 -> 15.65,
Wukong at 30, no GPU loss. A shader with indexed accesses takes ~27 %
longer for RADV to compile, once per cache.

